<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Cameroons</h1><p class="st-meta">1922 CE – 1961 CE · capital Buea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two strips of former German Kamerun ruled by Britain from Nigeria, whose 1961 plebiscites split them between Nigeria and Cameroon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took a narrow western portion of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a> in the division of 1916, confirmed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> in 1922 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory after 1946. It consisted of two separate areas: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cameroons">Northern Cameroons</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adamawa-emirate">Adamawa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a> as its centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Both parts were administered as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>, the north with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Region</a> and the south with the Eastern Region, an arrangement many southern Cameroonians resented. Former German plantations around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cameroon">Mount Cameroon</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a> Development Corporation. In 1954 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> gained its own legislature, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-endeley">Emmanuel Endeley</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ngu-foncha">John Ngu Foncha</a> as premier.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In UN plebiscites in February 1961 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cameroons">Northern Cameroons</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, which it did on 1 October 1961. The English-speaking legacy of the south remains a source of tension in Cameroon.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-cameroons.jpg" alt="British Cameroons" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Cameroon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1922 CE – 1961 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emmanuel Endeley, John Ngu Foncha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Pidgin, Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cameroons under British Mandate, Northern and Southern Cameroons</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=british-cameroons" aria-label="British Cameroons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4936 2022 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Cameroons lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5256 2278L5256 2278M5256 2278L4997 651M5256 2278L5094 2192M5256 2278L5382 1985M5256 2278L5295 2202M5256 2278L5280 2206M5256 2278L5278 2214"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5256 2278h0M4997 651h0M5094 2192h0M5382 1985h0M5295 2202h0M5280 2206h0M5278 2214h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5256 2278h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Buea. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=british-cameroons">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
